3.text:
In the just identified homoscedastic case with one instrument let

* $ \mu^{2}=n\pi ^{2}{\rm Var}(z)/\sigma _{v}^{2}$ be the concentration
parameter and
* $F$ the first–stage $F$ .

Up to the Nagar first order term

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]\approx 
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\,\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]=\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that with $k_{2}=1$

\begin{equation*}
\rho \; \equiv\; \frac{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}
{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}
\;\approx\;\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac{1}{F-1}\approx\frac1F ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the asymptotic Nagar relative bias is $1/\mu^{2}$ and in the
usual first–stage notation $\mu^{2}\simeq F-1$.  In the limit
$\nu_{2}\to\infty$

\begin{equation*}
F\;\xrightarrow{d}\;\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})=
\big(\sqrt{\mu^{2}}+Z\big)^{2},\qquad Z\sim N(0,1),
\end{equation*}
so that for a given true $\mu^{2}$ the $1-\alpha$ lower confidence
limit for $\mu^{2}$ is obtained from

\begin{equation*}
P\!\left(F\ge f_{obs}\mid\mu^{2}= \mu_{0}^{2}\right)=1-\alpha .
\end{equation*}
The $10\%$ Nagar relative bias is $\rho_{0}=0.10$, i.e. 
$\mu_{0}^{2}=1/\rho_{0}=10$.  Put $Y=\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}+Z\sim
N(\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}},1)$. Then

\begin{equation*}
P\!\left(F\le f\mid\mu_{0}^{2}\right)
 =P\!\left(|Y|\le \sqrt f\right)\approx
 \Phi\!\left(\sqrt f-\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}\right),
\end{equation*}
so that the $1-\alpha$ quantile of $F$ under $\mu_{0}^{2}=10$
is

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{f_{1-\alpha}}\;\approx\;\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}+z_{1-\alpha},
\qquad 
f_{1-\alpha}\approx\big(\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}+z_{1-\alpha}\big)^{2}.
\end{equation*}
With $z_{0.95}=1.645$,

\begin{equation*}
f_{0.95}\approx\Big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\Big)^{2}
          \approx(3.162+1.645)^{2}\approx 23.1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F_{obs}\gtrsim 23$ makes the 5 % lower confidence bound for
$\mu^{2}$ exceed $10$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{95\%}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{f_{0.95}}\approx0.10,
\end{equation*}
the usual $\approx 1/(F-1)$ form being $f_{0.95}+1\approx24.1$.
This is the Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal relative bias 2SLS critical
value, often quoted as $F\approx 16$–$23$ in the $F-1$–$F$ conventions.
In the $F-1\approx\mu^{2}$ convention the commonly quoted number is

$\;f_{0.95}\simeq 23\!-\!24\approx 23\;$ ,

about $F\approx24$ on the $F-1$ scale, which is the normal
$F>10$ rule made $95\%$–confident.  With the usual $1/F$ convention
this is sometimes quoted as the $F\approx16.4$ Stock–Yogo
$10\%$-relative-bias $5\%$-size critical value.

2.text:
Under the weak–instrument scaling $\pi=c/\sqrt n$ put
$\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ and $Q_{zz}=1$.  For the just identified
model

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{TSLS}-\beta=\frac{z'u}{z'z\pi+z'v}
\end{equation*}

so that, with the weak–instrument scaling, 

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{TSLS}-\beta\big]
       =-\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\,
       \frac{1}{\mu^{2}}+o(\mu^{-2}) ,
\end{equation*}
and, with the usual weak–instrument limit,
$\,{\rm plim}\,\big(\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big)=\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$.
Hence, up to the usual Nagar first order term,

\begin{equation*}
R(\mu^{2})\equiv 
\frac{\,{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{TSLS}-\beta\big]\,}
     {\,{\rm plim}\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]\,}
     = \frac{1}{\mu^{2}}+o(\mu^{-2}) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the 2SLS bias is the OLS bias divided by the concentration
parameter.  In large samples, with one instrument,

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}\;\approx\;\frac{\hat\pi^{2}}{\widehat{Var}(\hat\pi)}
       \;\approx\;1+\mu^{2},
\qquad 
T_{1}=\sqrt{F_{1}}\approx N\big(\delta,1\big),\;\;\delta=\sqrt{\mu^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

A $95\%$ one–sided lower confidence bound for $\delta$ is
$\delta_{L}=T_{1}-z_{0.95}$, $z_{0.95}=1.645$, i.e. for a  two–sided
$95\%$ interval $z_{0.975}=1.96$.  Putting the lower bound on the
concentration parameter at the $10\%$ relative–bias point
$R=0.10\iff\mu^{2}=10$,

\begin{equation*}
T_{1}-z_{0.95}\;>\;\sqrt{10}
\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\sqrt{F_{1}}\;>\;\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95},
\end{equation*}

so that, in the usual normal approximation for the first–stage $t$,

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}^{*}\approx\Big[\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\Big]^{2}
      \approx\Big[3.16+1.65\Big]^{2}\approx2.3\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F_{1}\approx 21\!-\!23$, the familiar Stock–Yogo $10\%$
maximal-relative-bias value.  With the conventional two-sided
$95\%$ normal critical value $z_{0.975}=1.96$,

\[
F_{1}^{*}\approx\big[\sqrt{10}+1.96\big]^{2}\approx 26.2,
\]

so that $F_{1}\gtrsim 25\!-\!27$, often quoted as $F\gtrsim 10$ on
average and $F\approx 22\!-\!26$ on a $95\%$ lower-confidence basis,
is the conventional $10\%$ relative-bias weak-instrument bound.  With
the one-sided $1.645$ normal approximation this is $F^{*}\approx23$,
essentially the $F\approx 22$ Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal-relative-bias
critical value, commonly rounded to the familiar $F\approx 10{-}11$
average rule and $F\approx 20\!-\!25$ on a $95\%$ confidence basis.
This is the usual $F>10$, $F\approx 22\!-\!26$ $95\%$ rule.  The
two–sided $1.96$ version is $\,F^{*}\sim26$, i.e. $\approx 23$ on the
one–sided $95\%$ lower bound, the value usually quoted as $F\approx
22\!-\!23$ in the literature.

